#v(0.8em)

=== §. 1. #emph[Of the apostle’s transition from general duties to particular ones.]

#smallcaps[There] are two callings to which it has pleased God to call every one. #emph[The one is general.] By virtue of it, certain common duties are required, which are to be performed by all men (such as knowledge, faith, obedience, repentance, love, mercy, justice, truth, etc.). #emph[The other is particular.] By virtue of it, certain special duties are required of separate persons, according to the distinct places in which the divine providence has set them, in commonwealth, church, or family. As there are these two, so God’s ministers ought to be careful to instruct God’s people in both kinds of duties: both those which concern their #emph[general] calling, and those also which concern their #emph[particular] calling. Accordingly Saint Paul (who, like Moses,#footnote[Num 12:7.] was “faithful in all the house of God”) first instructed God’s church sufficiently in those #emph[general] duties which belong to all Christians, of whatever sex, state, degree, or condition they may be. After that he goes on to lay down certain #emph[particular] duties, which belong to particular callings and conditions. Among these he chooses those which God has established in a family. With excellent skill he passes from those #emph[generals] to these #emph[particulars,] laying down a #emph[transition] between them in these words: “Submitting to one another in the fear of God.” These words refer both to what goes before and to what follows after. The form and manner in which this verse is set down, with a participle, #emph[submitting,] shows that it depends on what went before, and so refers to it. Again, the word itself is the very same as the one used #footnote[ὑποτασσόμενοι, submitting. ὑποτάσσεσθε, submit.] in the next verse. This shows that this verse contains the #emph[sum] of what follows, and so refers to it as a general refers to its particulars. This manner of passing from one point to another by a #emph[perfect transition,] which looks both ways, both to what is past and to what is coming, is very elegant, and it is also frequent with this apostle of ours. By it he teaches us #footnote[Note.] to give heed to what follows in such a way that we do not forget what is past. As we must give diligent attention to what remains, so we must retain well what we have heard, and not let it slip. Otherwise, if one precept makes another be forgotten (as one nail drives out another), it will be altogether in vain to add line to line, or precept to precept.

=== §. 2. #emph[Of joining service to men with our praise of God.]

As this verse refers to what was delivered before concerning our duty to God, it teaches us this lesson:

#emph[It is the duty of Christians, as to set forth the praise of God, so to be serviceable to one another.] For this purpose, in the #footnote[Doctrine.] Ten Commandments, the second table, which declares the service that we owe to one another, is added to the first table, which prescribes the duty that we owe to God. And he who said, “The first and great commandment is this: You shall love the Lord,”#footnote[Matt 22:38, 39.] etc., also said, “The second is like it: You shall love your neighbor,” etc. For this reason the #footnote[Heb 13:15, 16.] apostle, declaring what those sacrifices are with which God is well pleased, joins these two together: to give thanks to God, and to do good to man. The service which we perform to one another in the fear of God is a clear and real demonstration of the respect we bear to God. “Our goodness does not extend to God.”#footnote[Ps 16:2.] #footnote[Job 22:3; 35:7.] He is so high above us, so perfect and complete in himself, that we can neither give to him, nor can he receive from us. But in his own stead he has placed our brother, who is like ourselves. As we may do him hurt,#footnote[Job 35:8.] so by our faithful service we may do him much #footnote[Ps 16:3.] good, and in doing it God is much honored.

This exposes the hypocrisy of those who make a great show #footnote[1\. #emph[Use.] A mark of hypocrisy.] of praising God, and yet are scornful and disdainful to their brethren, and lazy to do any service to man. “These men’s religion is worthless.”#footnote[Jas 1:26. Isa 58:3 #emph[etc.] Mic 6:6. #emph[etc.] Matt 23:14. 1 John 4:20.] By this mark the prophets in their time, and Christ and his apostles in their time also, exposed the hypocrisy of those among whom they lived, and so may we also in our times. For there are many who often sing praise to God in their houses and in the midst of the congregation, and perform other parts of God’s outward worship, but toward one another are proud, haughty, envious, unmerciful, unjust, slanderous, and very slow to do any good service. Surely the outward service which they claim to perform to God does not so much wipe out the spot of profaneness, as their neglect of duty to man brands their foreheads with the stamp of hypocrisy. #footnote[2\. #emph[Use.] Do not put off one duty with another. Matt 23:23.]

For our part, let us not think to shift off one duty on the pretext of another, though that one may seem to be the weightier, lest that fearful #emph[woe] which Christ pronounced against the scribes and Pharisees fall on our heads. As God is careful to instruct us how to carry ourselves both toward his own Majesty and also toward one another, so let us approve ourselves to him in both. Let us remember what Christ said to the Pharisees: “These you#super[+] ought to have done, without neglecting the others.” The same Lord who requires praise to his own Majesty enjoins mutual service to one another. The neglect of this, as well as of that, shows too light a regard for his will and pleasure. “Therefore what God #footnote[Matt 19:6.] has joined together, let man not separate.”

=== §. 3. #emph[Of everyone’s submitting himself to another.]

Again, as this verse refers to what follows, it declares the general sum of all, which is to submit ourselves mutually to one another in the fear of God. Its parts are two:

+ An exhortation.
+ A direction.

In the exhortation are noted both the #emph[duty] itself, in this word #emph[submit,] and also the #emph[parties] to whom it is to be performed, #emph[one another.]

Both branches of the exhortation, that is, the #emph[duty] and the #emph[parties,] joined together, give us this doctrine:

#emph[It is a general, mutual duty belonging to all Christians] #footnote[2\. #emph[Doctrine.]] #emph[to submit themselves to one another.] For this precept is as general as any of the former ones, belonging to all sorts and degrees whatever, and this word #emph[one another] #footnote[ἀλλήλοις.] implies as much. In the same extent the apostle in another place exhorts us to “serve one another,” and again, every man to seek #footnote[Gal 5:13. 1 Cor 10:24.] “the good of others.”

Concerning #emph[inferiors,] it is clear without question that #footnote[Question.] they ought to submit themselves to their superiors. Indeed, concerning #emph[equals] no great question can be raised: they must go before one another in giving honor,#footnote[Rom 12:10. How superiors ought to submit themselves.] and so submit themselves. But concerning #emph[superiors,] a fair question may be raised, whether it is a duty required of them to submit themselves to their inferiors.

To resolve this doubt, we must first distinguish between #footnote[Answer.] the subjection of #emph[reverence] and the subjection of #emph[service.]

#emph[Subjection of reverence] is that by which one acknowledges a preeminence and superiority in those whom he reverences. He does it in speech, by giving them titles of honor; or in gesture, by some kind of bow; or in action, by readily obeying their commands. This belongs to inferiors only.

#emph[Subjection of service] is that by which one, in his place, is #footnote[The difference between subjection of #emph[reverence] and of #emph[service.]] ready to do what good he can to another. This is common to all Christians. It is a duty which even superiors owe to inferiors, according to the extent of this word #emph[one another] named before. In this respect even the highest governor on earth is called a #emph[minister,] for the good of those who #footnote[διάκονος. Rom 13:4.] are under him.

Secondly, we must make a difference between the #emph[work] itself#footnote[The difference between the #emph[work] and the #emph[manner] of doing it.] and the #emph[manner] of doing it. A work which in itself is a work of superiority and authority may, in the manner of doing it, be a work of submission: that is, if it is done in humility and meekness of mind. The magistrate, by ruling with meekness and humility, submits himself to his subject. In this respect the apostle exhorts, “Do #emph[nothing]” (no, not the highest and greatest works that #footnote[Phil 2:3.] can be) “out of empty pride, but in humility.”

Thirdly, we must distinguish between the different places in which men stand. For even those who are superiors to some #footnote[Every one is under some authority. Matt 8:9.] are inferiors to others, like the one who said, “I myself am a man under authority, with soldiers under me.” The master who has servants under him may be under the authority of a magistrate. Indeed, God has so ordered each one’s place that there is not any one who is not in some respect under another. The wife, though a mother of children, is under her husband. The husband, though head of a family, is under public magistrates. Public magistrates are one under another, and all under the king. The king himself is under God, and under his word delivered by his ambassadors, to which the highest are to submit themselves. And ministers of the word, as subjects, are under their kings and governors. He who says, “Everyone must submit himself to the governing authorities,” #footnote[Rom 13:1.] does not except ministers of the word. And he who says, “Obey your#super[+] leaders and submit to them,” #footnote[Heb 13:17. The difference between the authority of magistrates and of ministers.] does not except kings. The only difference is this: the authority of the king is in himself, and in his own name he may command obedience to be performed to himself; but the authority of a minister is in Christ, and only in Christ’s name may he require obedience to be performed to Christ.

The reason why all are bound to submit themselves to one #footnote[#emph[Reason.] Each one’s office is for the good of another.] another is this: every one is set in his place by God, not so much for himself as for the good of others. For this reason the apostle exhorts, “No one should seek his own good, but the good of others.”#footnote[1 Cor 10:24.] Even governors are raised to places of dignity and authority more for the good of their subjects than for their own honor. Their callings are in truth offices of service, indeed burdens, to which they must willingly put their shoulders, being called by God. And they must give an account of them, concerning the good which they have done to others. To bring that good about, they need to submit themselves.

Therefore let every one, high and low, rich and poor, #footnote[1\. #emph[Use.] An exhortation to all to do service.] superior and inferior, magistrate and subject, minister and people, husband and wife, parent and child, master and servant, neighbors and fellows, all of all sorts in their different places, take notice of their duty in this point of submission, and make it a matter of conscience to put it into practice. Let magistrates do it by securing the welfare and peace of their people, as Mordecai did;#footnote[Esth 10:3.] ministers, by making themselves servants to their people, not seeking their own profit but the profit of many, that they may be saved, as Paul did;#footnote[1 Cor 9:19; 10:33.] fathers, by bringing up their children well, and taking heed that they do not provoke them to wrath, as David did;#footnote[Prov 4:3, 4.] husbands, by living with their wives with understanding, giving honor to the wife as to the weaker vessel, as Abraham did; #footnote[Gen 16:6.] masters, by doing what is just and fair to their servants, as the centurion did.#footnote[Luke 7:2.] Let every one do it by being of the same mind toward one another, and by serving one another in love, according to the apostle’s rule.#footnote[Rom 12:16. Gal 5:13.] Let this duty of submission first be well learned, and then all other duties will be better performed.

Do not be high-minded, nor swell against one another. #footnote[2\. #emph[Use.] A warning against swelling against one another.] Though in outward condition some may be higher than others, yet in Christ all are one, whether slave or free;#footnote[Gal 3:28.] all are members of one and the same body.#footnote[1 Cor 12:27.] Now consider the mutual affection (if I may so speak) of the members of a natural body toward one another. Not one of them will puff itself up and rise against another. The head, which is the highest and of greatest honor, will submit itself to the feet in performing the duty of a head, as well as the feet to the head in performing their duty; and so with all the other parts. Nor is it implied by this that those who are in a place of dignity and authority should forget or give up their place, dignity, or authority, and become like inferiors under authority, any more than the head does. For the head, in submitting itself, does not go on the ground and bear the body, as the feet do; but it submits itself by directing and governing the other parts, and that with all the humility, meekness, and gentleness that it can. So must all superiors do. Much more must equals and inferiors learn to perform their duty with humility and meekness,#footnote[Eph 4:2.] without scorn or disdain. This is what the apostle expressly mentioned before, and here hints at again: none are exempted or excused from it. We know that it is unnatural and unbecoming for the head to scorn the feet and to swell against them, but it is worse than monstrous for one hand to scorn another. What then shall we say if the feet swell against the head? Surely such scorn and disdain among the members would cause not only great disturbance, but also utter ruin to the body. And can it be otherwise in a political body? But on the contrary, when all of all sorts willingly submit themselves to one another (as has been shown before), the whole body and every member of it will reap good by it. Indeed, by this mutual submission, as we do good, so we shall receive good.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of the fear of God.]

So much for the #emph[exhortation.] The #emph[direction] follows: #emph[In the fear of the Lord.] This clause is added to declare partly the #emph[means] by which men may be brought to submit themselves readily to one another, and partly the #emph[manner] in which they ought to submit themselves. #emph[The fear of the Lord] is both the #emph[efficient cause] that moves a true Christian willingly to perform every duty to man, and also the #emph[end] to which he refers everything that he does. For the better understanding of this, I will briefly declare

+ What this fear of the Lord is.
+ How the Lord is the proper object of it.
+ What its extent is.
+ Why it is so much urged.

First, the fear of God is a reverent awe of the divine Majesty. #footnote[1\. What the fear of God is. The causes of it.] At times it arises from faith in the mercy and goodness of God. For when the heart of man has once felt a sweet taste of God’s goodness, and found that all happiness consists in his favor alone, it is struck with such an inward awe and reverence that it would not displease #footnote[Two effects of a filial fear of God.] his Majesty for anything, but would rather do whatever it may know to be pleasing and acceptable to him. For these are the two effects which arise from this kind of fear of God:

1\. #emph[A careful endeavor to please God.] In this respect #footnote[#emph[Deum timere est nulla mala facere, & nulla bona quae facienda sunt praeterire. Bernar. de modo benè viuen. ser.] 4. \[To fear God is to do no evil, and to pass over no good thing that ought to be done.\]] good King Jehoshaphat, having exhorted his judges to execute the judgment of the Lord rightly, adds this clause as a motive to it: “May the fear of the #smallcaps[Lord] be upon you#super[+].”#footnote[2 Chr 19:6, 7.] By this he implied that God’s fear would make them endeavor to approve themselves to God.

2\. #emph[A careful avoiding of such things as offend the Majesty of God] and grieve his Spirit. In this respect the wise man says, “To fear the #smallcaps[Lord] is to hate evil.”#footnote[Prov 8:13.] And of Job it is said that he was “fearing God and shunning evil.” #footnote[Job 1:1.]

At other times, again, awe and dread of the divine Majesty arise from distrust. For when a man’s heart doubts God’s mercy and expects nothing but vengeance, the very thought of God strikes an awe, or rather a dread, into him, and so makes him fear God.

From this double cause of fear, the one contrary #footnote[The difference between filial and servile fear.] to the other, has arisen that common distinction between a #emph[filial] or son-like fear and a #emph[servile] or slavish fear. This distinction is grounded on these words of the apostle: “You#super[+] did not receive a spirit of slavery that returns you to fear” (this is #footnote[Bernard. loc. citat.] a servile fear), “but you received the Spirit of sonship, #footnote[Rom 8:15.] by whom we cry, ‘Abba! Father!’” This causes a filial fear. #emph[Filial] fear is the kind of fear that dutiful children bear toward their fathers. But #emph[servile] fear is the kind that slaves bear toward their masters. A son fears simply to offend or displease his father, so that #footnote[#emph[Est timor ne amittatur gratia beneficij. Hic timor castus est, non eum charitas eijcit, sed asciscit. August. epist.] 120. \[There is a fear lest the grace of the gift be lost. This fear is chaste; love does not cast it out, but takes it in.\]] his fear is accompanied with love. A slave fears nothing but the punishment of his offense, so that his fear is joined with hatred; such a one does not fear to sin, but fears to burn in hell for sin. Faithful Abraham, like a gracious child, feared God (as God’s angel bears witness, Gen 22:12) when he was ready to sacrifice his only son rather than offend God by refusing to #footnote[#emph[Qui gehennas metuit, non peccare metuit sed ardere. Aug. epist.] 144.] obey his command. But faithless Adam, like a servile slave, feared God (as he himself testifies against himself, Gen 3:10) when, after he had broken God’s command, he hid himself from the presence of God. This slavish fear is plainly a devilish fear (for the devils #footnote[Jas 2:19.] fear so that they tremble). It makes men wish there were no hell, no day #emph[of judgment,] no judge, indeed no God. This is the fear #emph[without which] we must serve the Lord.#footnote[Luke 1:74.] To submit oneself in this fear is not at all acceptable to God. It is therefore the #emph[filial fear] which is meant here.

Secondly, God is the proper #emph[object] of this fear, as is evident from #footnote[2\. How God is the proper object of fear.] this testimony and many more in Scripture, where the fear of #emph[God] and of #emph[the Lord] is mentioned. This fear has so proper a relation to God that the Scripture, by a kind of special right, gives God this title, #emph[Fear.] For where Jacob mentions “the Fear of Isaac,”#footnote[Gen 31:42, 53.] he means the Lord whom Isaac feared.

Is it then unlawful to fear any but God? #footnote[Question.]

No. Men also may be feared, such as princes, parents, masters, #footnote[#emph[Answer.] How men are to be feared.] and other superiors. For the apostle, exhorting us to give every one his due,#footnote[Rom 13:7.] gives this instance: “fear to whom fear is due.” Yet God may nevertheless be said to be the proper object of fear, because all the fear that is in any way due to any creature is due to him in and for the Lord, whose image he carries. So in truth it is not so much the person of a man that is to be feared, as the image of God placed in him by virtue of some authority or dignity belonging to him. If any such opposition should arise between God and man that in fearing man our fear would be withdrawn from God, then the rule of Christ is to prevail, which is this: “Do not fear those who #footnote[Matt 10:28.] kill the body but cannot kill the soul. Instead, fear the One who can destroy both soul and body in hell.”

Thirdly, the extent of this true filial fear of God is #footnote[3\. Every duty is to be seasoned with the fear of God.] very large. No one point throughout the whole Scripture is urged more than this #emph[fear of the Lord.] It is often added to other duties, as that by which they are seasoned, and without which they cannot be performed well. That is why we are commanded to “serve the #smallcaps[Lord] with fear,”#footnote[Ps 2:11.] to be “perfecting holiness in the fear of God,”#footnote[2 Cor 7:1.] and to work out our salvation “with fear.”#footnote[Phil 2:12.] And the churches are commended for “living in the fear of the Lord,”#footnote[Acts 9:31.] and so likewise particular men, such as Abraham,#footnote[Gen 22:12.] Joseph,#footnote[Gen 42:18.] Job,#footnote[Job 1:1.] and many others. Indeed, the whole worship of God is often summed up under this branch of #emph[fear.] So when our Savior Christ cites this text, “Fear the #smallcaps[Lord] your God,” #footnote[Deut 6:13.] he expresses it this way: “Worship the Lord your God.”#footnote[Matt 4:10.] And again, where the Lord says by his prophet Isaiah, “Their fear #footnote[Isa 29:13.] of Me is but rules taught by men,” Christ quotes that text this way: “They worship Me in vain; they teach as doctrine #footnote[Matt 15:9.] the precepts of men.” From these places compared together it is evident that under the #emph[fear] of God is included the #emph[worship] of God. Indeed, all the duty which we owe to God and man is summed up under this title, #emph[the fear of God.] For when David would declare in one word the sum and substance of all that a minister ought to teach his people, he says, “I will teach you#super[+] the fear of #footnote[Ps 34:11.] the #smallcaps[Lord].” #footnote[4\. Why the fear of God is so much urged.]

Fourthly, the reason why the Holy Spirit so much urges #emph[the fear of God,] and that in so large an extent as has been shown, I take to be this: to show a difference between the integrity and perfection of God’s image which was first planted in man at his creation, and the renewal of it while he lives here in this world. So complete and perfect was God’s image in man then that he needed no other motive but #emph[love] to stir him up to any duty. That is why, when the Holy Spirit would set forth that perfection of God’s image first planted in man, he adds this title #emph[love] to other duties, whether they concern God or man. Concerning God, Moses exhorts Israel “to love the #footnote[Deut 11:13.] #smallcaps[Lord] your God and to serve Him,” and again, “to love the #smallcaps[Lord] your God, to walk in #footnote[Deut 30:16.] His ways, and to keep His commandments,” etc. Concerning man, the apostle exhorts us to “serve one another in love,”#footnote[Gal 5:13.] and to “do everything in love.”#footnote[1 Cor 16:14.] Indeed, at times the Holy Spirit is pleased to sum up all duties under #emph[love.] In this respect Christ calls this commandment (“You shall love the Lord”) “the #footnote[Matt 22:38.] great commandment,” which includes all the commandments of the first table under it. And for the second table, Saint Paul says that “love is the fulfillment of the law.”#footnote[Rom 13:10.] But by Adam’s fall, and the corruption with which it infected man’s nature, the love of God has grown cold in man. And though the saints are created anew according to that image of God, yet while they live in this world that image is not as perfect as it was; the flesh remains in the best of them. In this respect God has firmly fixed this feeling of #emph[fear] in man’s heart, and by it he both restrains him from sin and also stirs him up to every good duty.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of the fear of God moving us to do service to men.]

Having briefly declared the #emph[nature, object, extent,] and #emph[use] of fear, I return to the point in hand, that is, to show 1. how it is laid down here as a motive to stir men up to perform the duty required here. For by this clause, #emph[in the fear of the Lord,] the apostle implies that

#emph[It is the fear of God which moves men to submit] #footnote[Doctrine.] #emph[themselves to one another as a matter of conscience.] This made David#footnote[2 Sam 23:3.] rule the people of God so well, and Joseph#footnote[Gen 42:18.] deal so well with his brothers. Indeed, #footnote[Isa 11:2, 3.] this is noted as the cause of the righteous rule of Christ himself. That good King Jehoshaphat knew this well. Therefore, when he appointed judges over his people, he said to them, as a motive to stir them up to execute the judgments of the Lord rightly, “May the fear of the #smallcaps[Lord] be upon you#super[+].”#footnote[2 Chr 19:7.] So also Saint Peter, to move subjects to honor their king, sets this exhortation first: “Fear God.” #footnote[1 Pet 2:17.]

By fear of man one may be brought to submit himself #footnote[Objection.] to another. A magistrate may be moved to deal justly and mildly with his people through fear of insurrections and rebellions. Subjects may be brought to submit themselves by severe laws and tyranny. And so may other inferiors also, by threats, by harsh treatment, and by other self-serving motives.

1\. Though fear of man is a motive, it does not follow #footnote[Answer.] that the fear of God is therefore no motive. It may be another motive, and a better motive.

2\. The submission which is performed through fear of #footnote[The difference between doing a thing for fear of God and for fear of man.] man is a forced and slavish submission, not at all acceptable to God. But the submission which is performed through a true filial fear of God is a free, willing, ready, cheerful, conscientious submission. It is such a submission as will stir us up to do by it the best good we can to those to whom we submit ourselves. So it is more acceptable to God, by reason of its cause, and more profitable to man, by reason of its effect and fruit.

For a true fear of God makes us regard what #footnote[#emph[Reason.] The power of the fear of God.] God requires and commands more than what our corrupt heart desires and suggests. It subdues our unruly passions and brings them within the bounds of duty. It makes us deny ourselves and our own desires. And though through the corruption of our nature and our inborn pride we are loath to submit, yet God’s fear will bring down that proud mind, and make us humble and gentle. It will keep those who are in authority from tyranny, cruelty, and excessive severity. And it will keep those who are under subjection from pretense, deceit, and secret conspiracies.

See how necessary it is that a true fear of the Lord #footnote[#emph[Use.] The necessity of the fear of God.] be planted in men’s hearts: in the hearts of kings and all governors, in the hearts of subjects and all people, whether superiors or inferiors. Where there is no fear of God, there will be no good submission to man. Abraham #footnote[Gen 20:11.] thought that the men of Gerar would have no respect for him or his wife, nor make a matter of conscience of common uprightness, nor keep from shedding innocent blood, because he saw that there was “no fear of God in this place.” And the apostle, having listed #footnote[Rom 3:11, #emph[etc.]] many notorious effects of man’s natural corruption, concludes them all with this, as the cause of all: “There is no fear of God before their eyes.” Therefore let magistrates, parents, masters, and all in authority take special care that their subjects, children, servants, and all under them may be taught and brought to fear the Lord. I dare affirm that inferiors who are taught to fear God will do better service to their superiors than those who fear their superiors only as men, and do not fear God. Let ministers especially urge and press a fear of God on the consciences of men. Let all inferiors pray that the fear of the Lord may be planted in the hearts of their superiors, so that they may live a quiet and peaceable life under them in all godliness and dignity. Happy is that kingdom where magistrates and subjects fear the Lord. Happy is that church where ministers and people fear the Lord. Happy is that family where husband and wife, parents and children, master and servants fear the Lord. In such a kingdom, church, and family, every one will submit himself to the others, for the mutual good of one another. But if those who do not fear God submit themselves, whether they are superiors or inferiors, it is for their own ends and advantages, and not for the good of those to whom they submit themselves.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of limiting all duty to man within the bounds of the fear of God.]

Again, as this clause #emph[(In the fear of the Lord)] declares the manner of submission, it shows that

#emph[No submission is to be performed to man except that which] #footnote[Doctrine.] #emph[may stand with the fear of God.] By this we show that we have respect to God, and labor above all to approve ourselves to him. So David is commanded to “rule in the #footnote[2 Sam 23:3.] fear of God,” and other magistrates to perform their duty #footnote[2 Chr 19:9.] “in the fear of the #smallcaps[Lord]”; and Nehemiah, that good governor, #footnote[Neh 5:15.] was careful to do so. So also subjects are to obey in the #emph[fear of the Lord.] The apostle implies this by setting #footnote[1 Pet 2:17.] this precept, “Fear God,” before that one, “honor the king”; as if he had said, “So honor the king that in it, and by it, you#super[+] may show your fear of God. Do not let this latter cross the former.” Servants likewise are commanded to be obedient to their masters with this proviso: “fearing the Lord.”#footnote[Col 3:22.] Such phrases as these, “for the Lord’s sake,” “as to the Lord,” “in the #footnote[1 Pet 2:13.] Lord,” “as servants of Christ,” and the like, joined to #footnote[Eph 5:22; 6:1, 6.] the duties of inferiors, imply as much.

There is great reason that all service should be limited #footnote[1\. #emph[Reason.] God the highest Lord.] by the #emph[fear of God.] For God is the highest Lord, to whom all service is due first and chiefly. Whatever service is due to any man, high or low, is due in and for the Lord. The Lord has set superiors in their places of preeminence, in which they bear the image of God. The Lord has also set inferiors in their places, and entrusted them as his charge to the government of those who are over them. He who does not obey those who are over him #emph[in the fear of God] shows no respect for God’s image. And he who does not govern those who are under him #emph[in the fear of God] shows no respect for God’s charge.

Besides, God is that great Judge to whom all of all sorts, #footnote[2\. #emph[Reason.] God the Judge of all.] superiors and inferiors, must give an account of their service. However well we have approved ourselves to men by our service, yet if we have not had respect to God in it, and approved ourselves to him, with what face can we appear before his dreadful judgment seat? #footnote[1\. #emph[Use.] The folly of all those who fear man more than God.] Can the favor of those whom we have pleased in this world protect and shelter us from the fury of God’s displeasure?

See the folly of those governors who wholly give themselves to the whims of their people, even though it is against the Lord and his word. This was Adam’s folly, who #footnote[Gen 3:6.] ate of the forbidden fruit at his wife’s urging. This was Aaron’s folly, who set up an #footnote[Exod 32:1.] idol to please the people. And this was Saul’s folly, who, against God’s express #footnote[1 Sam 15:21.] prohibition, allowed his people to take some of the spoil of the Amalekites. The same may be said of Joash, who #footnote[2 Chr 24:17.] listened to his princes and set up idols, and of Pilate, who, #footnote[Mark 15:15.] to please the people, delivered Christ to be crucified against his conscience. The fearful outcome of this submission of theirs, not seasoned with the fear of God but contrary to it, may be a warning to all superiors to take heed how they seek to please those who are under them more than God who is above them. The outcome of Adam’s, Aaron’s, Saul’s, and Joash’s base submission is noted by the Holy Spirit in their separate histories. Of Pilate it is recorded that, being #footnote[#emph[Euseb. hist. Eccl. lib] 2. #emph[cap.] 7.] brought into extreme distress, he laid violent hands on himself.

Nor is it to be counted folly only in superiors to submit themselves to their inferiors against the Lord, but also in inferiors to submit to their superiors against him. For by it they show that they fear man more than God, which Christ expressly #footnote[Luke 12:4, 5.] forbids his friends to do. The captains who went #footnote[2 Kgs 1:9, #emph[etc.]] to fetch Elijah obeyed their king in it; but what did they gain by it? Was the king able to save them from the fire which God sent down from heaven on them? The women #footnote[Jer 44:19.] who were rebuked for burning incense to the Queen of Heaven did not do it without their husbands; yet they were not excused by that. The children and others in the family #footnote[Num 16:32.] submitted themselves to Dathan and Abiram in standing at the door of their tents in defiance of Moses; but because it was not in the Lord, but against him, they were not exempted from the judgment. Therefore let all of all sorts set the #emph[fear of God] before them as a mark to aim at in all their actions. Let superiors #footnote[Num 11:29.] neither do anything to please their inferiors, #footnote[1 Sam 24:8; 26:9.] nor allow anything to be done for their sakes by their inferiors, which cannot stand with the #emph[fear of God.] And let inferiors #footnote[Gen 39:10.] neither do, #footnote[1 Sam 22:17.] #footnote[Acts 4:19.] nor forbear to do, at the will of their superiors, anything that swerves from the fear of God. But let every one “submit to one another in the fear of God.”
